Sun-Escapement

Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Tuba + 2020s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: Handcrafted brass rotary valves and kinetic rhythm generators forged from fallen nickel-iron meteorites and salvaged 19th-century colonial clockwork.

Sun-Escapement is a luminous, sun-drenched fusion of modern broken-beat nu-jazz and Caribbean syncopation, anchored by the seismic, breathy warmth of an acoustic tuba operating as an elastic sub-bass synth. Complex horological gear-mechanisms provide crisp, micro-timed metallic percussion over breezy electric piano voicings, creating a rhythm section that sounds simultaneously ancient, mechanical, and ecstatically alive.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 118–126 BPM. A forward-leaning, syncopated swing borrowing equally from West London broken beat and Martinican bèlè, featuring syncopated clockwork subdivisions and half-time tuba drops.
- Harmonic Palette: Lydian dominant and Dorian modes rich with major 9th, 11th, and suspended chord clusters; lush, sunlit harmonic voicings inspired by tropicalia, bossa nova, and 2020s UK jazz.
- Production Signatures: Close-mic’d rotary-valve tuba processed through analog tape saturation to dominate the 40–90 Hz band without synthetic sub distortion; micro-sampled mechanical gear escapements and ticking balance-wheels functioning as crisp hi-hats; hyper-clean modern sidechain ducking that lets ocean-breeze field recordings and brass stabs breathe together.
- Dynamics: Tracks typically open with airy, unmetered Fender Rhodes vamps and ticking rubato clockwork before detonating into full-throttle brass funk; dynamic drops strip the arrangement down to a lone chattering tuba and ticking escapement gears before rebuilding into radiant polyrhythmic climaxes.

Scene & Origin Lore
Sun-Escapement coalesced in the open-air harbor bistros and repair docks of Saint-Pierre and Marseille between 2022 and 2024. A close-knit collective of Antillean brass players and horology restorers discovered that custom tuba valves sleeved in nickel-iron fragments of the 1932 Mount Pelée meteorite eliminated friction, yielding an impossibly fast, resonant low register when paired with escapement springs from salvaged tower clocks. The scene quickly adopted an aesthetic of breezy utilitarian elegance: crisp linen worker-smocks worn over watchmaker aprons, brass-rimmed sea shades, and pocket watch chains clipped to modern modular synthesizers. Sunrise dance rituals known as "the winding" see crowds gather along salt-sprayed seawalls as tubists lubricate their meteorite valves with coconut-mineral oil to jam against solar-powered drum sequencers in the blinding Atlantic dawn.

Typical Instruments: Meteorite-valved BBb acoustic tuba, Fender Rhodes Mark 7, antique clockwork gear idiophones, soprano and tenor saxophones, steel pan, vibraphone, cuíca, Prophet-6 analog synthesizer, and modern hardware samplers (Elektron Octatrack).

For Fans Of: Sons of Kemet, Kaidi Tatham, Meridian Brothers, Kamaal Williams.

