Track 2: "Why Keep the Envelope Sealed?"
- Story & Context: In her cramped attic workshop overlooking the Fort-de-France docks, master horologist Mireille Laurent spent three months disassembling an 1880s escapement escapement-wheel to rebuild the sluggish low-valve of Émile Vaneau’s meteorite-plated tuba. She found a scorched, unsent love letter wedged inside the clock’s brass housing, its lavender ink faded by a century of humid island trade winds, confessing a secret liaison that ended in ruin before the great volcanic blast. Rather than opening the inner fold, Mireille welded the brass casing shut around the letter's iron meteorite clasp and sang its unopened sorrow over a buoyant, double-time bossa-funk tempo: "You let the harbor rust while you held your tongue." Her trembling alto vocal floats directly over Émile’s warm, thunderous tuba stabs, turning private cowardice into the coast’s most infectious summer heartbreak groove.
